<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Kage macOS entitlements, embedded into the signed .app by tauri-bundler.
  Wired via `bundle.macOS.entitlements` in ../../tauri.conf.json.

  Only used when the build is code-signed (APPLE_SIGNING_IDENTITY set, see
  docs/RELEASE.md, "macOS code signing"). Unsigned builds carry no
  entitlements. tauri-bundler applies this SAME file to every target it signs:
  each externalBin sidecar (kage-computer-control-mcp, kage-calendar-helper),
  the main binary and the .app.

  Hardened runtime is ON (bundle.macOS.hardenedRuntime = true). Why:
    * Notarization requires it, so leaving it on keeps the Developer ID +
      notarization path open with no further changes.
    * Nothing Kage does in-process needs a runtime exception:
      - Agent CLIs, the MCP/calendar sidecars, python (pocket-tts), osascript
        and the pip installer are separate executables started via
        std::process::Command. Hardened runtime limits only the signed process
        itself, never its children.
      - No in-process JIT: WKWebView runs JavaScript (including the extension
        sandbox and graphviz wasm) in Apple's WebContent XPC process, so no
        allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory.
      - No third-party dylibs: the Rust binaries are statically linked and link
        only system frameworks (AppKit, EventKit, CoreGraphics, Accessibility),
        and nothing dlopen()s at runtime. Library validation therefore never
        triggers, so no disable-library-validation. That one would also let
        injected code run with Kage's TCC grants, so keep it off.
      - No DYLD_* variables are needed, so no allow-dyld-environment-variables.
    * What hardened runtime DOES gate are the TCC-protected resources below.
      Each needs its entitlement or TCC denies access silently, with no
      prompt. Accessibility, Input Monitoring and Screen Recording are
      TCC-only (no entitlement exists for them).
  Add an entitlement here only when Kage actually uses it. Hardened runtime
  denies anything not listed.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- osascript / System Events: window list, focus, selected-text capture. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- Voice input: getUserMedia in the webviews (ui/js/shared/echo-canceller.js). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- EventKit in the kage-calendar-helper sidecar (requestFullAccessToEvents).
         Pairs with NSCalendars*UsageDescription in Info.plist. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
    <true/>

    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
